WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.920
Imagine how you learn in school.

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:05.800
One teacher gives the same
demonstration to everyone.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:09.740
During the lesson, different students
may all look like they fully understand.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.100
However, the actual performance can
only be tested beyond the lecture.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:18.540
Let's pick student A, give a real exam
on the same task and watch the rollouts.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.320
How can we improve
student A's performance?

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.920
Give more demonstrations?

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:26.360
Tell only yes or no.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:28.540
Or give an overall reward score.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:32.460
But a score without step info
might confuse the student.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.160
The reward may work after
tuning between A and teacher.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:39.700
But when using the same method
for B or C, it may not work.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:43.380
In school, teachers do not set
rewards differently for everyone.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:45.400
They set a standard trial evaluation.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.660
Now consider the same idea in robotics.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:53.020
Each VLA model is a different student to
learn from the teacher's demonstrations.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:56.520
We should also evaluate its
performance through multiple trials.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:01.400
Inspired by human education, we are
considering the same for robotic learning.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:04.900
Learn from useful rollouts and
corrections and avoid bad behaviors

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:08.080
while preserving each model's
learning and behavior.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.540
Motivated by this, we introduce PACER,

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:16.780
a process-aware VLA policy
learning from human corrections,

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:19.140
and apply it to robotic disassembly.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.400
As shown here, after deployment,
the VLA rollout can be successful,

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.480
near the target, moving toward a
wrong location, or completely failed.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:30.300
So the key question is not simply
how to filter success and failure,

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:34.020
and not how to learn a private
reward model to tune one VLA.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:37.260
Instead, we want the model to
learn from its own rollout process,

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.380
just like the student learns from an exam.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:42.500
What went well, what went wrong,
and what can be corrected.

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.100
The PACER pipeline starts
from the rollout traces

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:50.260
and human corrections collected
by deploying the initial policy.

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:52.220
Each trace is labeled and gated,

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:56.020
so unsafe or clearly wrong attempts
are never used for imitation.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.840
The remaining traces are
compiled into action chunks,

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:00.900
and each chunk receives process evidence,

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:04.560
such as progress toward the target
or entry into the terminal region.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:06.740
This evidence sets how strongly each chunk

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:09.320
contributes to the native
VLA training objective,

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:12.400
while the backbone and
loss remain unchanged.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.740
After initial SFT, the
policy runs rollout trials.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.720
Successful trials are kept as traces.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:22.120
For failures, the operator
records a correction,

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:25.520
the arm returns home, and then
provides a clean demonstration.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:32.680
We first compare the initial
SFT policy, vanilla post-SFT,

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:37.760
and the selected PACER model on two
representative tasks, CPU fan and RAM.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:46.600
Next, we compare three ways
to use post-training traces.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:50.440
Outcome-only learning, fixed geometry
weighting, and PACER selected.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:02:59.240
Finally,

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:05.080
we show the selected PACER policy in a
demonstrated robotic disassembly workflow.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:08.880
This final demo summarizes
the main idea of PACER.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:13.000
By learning from its own rollout
process and human corrections,

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.460
the VLA policy can improve its behavior

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:20.800
while still using the original VLA
backbone and native training objective.
